[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The DSL's named constructors cover the common case, not the relation space

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-NAMED-CONSTRUCTORS-DO-NOT-SPAN
TITLE: The DSL's named constructors cover the common case, not the relation space
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The settled DSL shape gives rel.parent and rel.child two positional roles, and
rel.file as a bare value carrying none. The grammar allows more than that in
both directions: ROLE and REVERSE_ROLE are optional on Parent and Child, so a
role-less parent relation is legal, and File takes a ROLE even though it has no
REVERSE_ROLE production at all.

So the three named constructors between them cannot write two shapes the
grammar accepts. Both are reachable through the generic rel.mk -- rel.mk
"parent" {} and rel.mk "file" {role = "Depends";} -- and the types accept both,
so nothing is unwritable. What is missing is a named way to say it.

Whether that matters depends on whether the DSL is meant to be the readable
surface for every legal grammar or the fast path for the common one. The same
question applies to HUMAN_TITLE, which no field constructor names either.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noted while implementing the DSL against the settled shape. The gap is easy to
mistake for an oversight and rewrite, and the fix -- more arities, or optional
attrset arguments -- costs the property that every constructor is currently a
one-line attrset literal.
<<<
